  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\n\nThe aforementioned sfc high should be sitting over the mid-Missouri \nValley area by Thursday morning. Some guidance tries to spark off \nshowers or isolated storms in northeast Nebraska during the morning \nand afternoon hours, largely forced by some H8 WAA rounding the \nbackside of the high. However, activity may struggle to reach the \nsfc given the dry low level air seen in forecast BUFKIT soundings. \nHighs warm to the 70s.\n\nAn H8 baroclinic zone will swing through the area early Friday \nmorning. Strong WAA and a 30 to 40 kt LLJ could help force some \nshowers or storms. With this forecast update, PoPs of 15 to 30% are \nforecast for early Friday morning primarily northeast of a line from \nVerdigre to Tekamah to Missouri Valley. Highs rebound to the low \n80s. Expect to see some breeziness too as a tight pressure gradient \nsets up ahead of our next approaching system.\n\nModels depict the H5 pattern with an intensifying longwave trof \nacross the western CONUS on Saturday. Just ahead of the feature, \nshould see a sfc low spin up and track northeast into the Dakotas \nand Minnesota. While the feature stays well north of the area, the \ntight pressure gradient and PBL mixing into a 45 to 50 kt H8 LLJ \nwill result in windy conditions. Expect gusts of 40 to 45 mph, and a \nfew isolated gusts up to 50 mph are not out of the question, \nparticularly in far northeast Nebraska. The bulk of GEFS and EPS \nensembles suggest gusts of 40 to 45 mph across northeast Nebraska \ntoo. Will need to keep an eye on fire weather that day, especially \nin northeast Nebraska where we could see Red Flag Warning conditions \nmet. Expect another warm day with highs in the 80s.\n\nTemperatures cool slightly for Sunday before another front moves \nthrough Monday, cooling temperatures to the 60s and 70s. Precip \nchances arrive too for Monday at 15 to 20%, but considerable \nspread is seen amongst model guidance regarding timing and \nplacement of features.",
